hi everyone my name is Sam and I'm a  graduate student in comparative Media  Studies I have a background in animation  and public health research and I'm  fascinated by people who can take big  complex history topics and offer them up  for discussion and artistic formats so  I'm really excited to be speaking with  Rosa today for a cast conversation and  love to give her a chance for a self  introduction hey Rosa  and I am an illustrator comic artist  from Puerto Rico I live in someone and  I've been self-publishing comics for ten  years and recently I've switched over to  political comics where I I tried to  write a comic about our experiences in  the island or politics and all the  problems that have surfaced after  Hurricane Maria specifically and I  usually published mostly on the NAMM  book but I've also been published on the  believer and the lily for The Washington  Post so my first question sort of brings  us here today is pretty basic but what  brought you to MIT and how is it  influencing your work well I was invited  by cast and mikail Jacobson to work for  the MIT game lab on illustrations for a  board game based on promise that the  promesa law in Puerto Rico so the  promise a law is a law that President  Obama signed a way back in 2016 and it  imposes a fiscal oversight and control  board over our government and it's been  a huge of course it's been a huge issue  here and I wrote a little bit about it  on my comics and so Michael contacted me  based on that because they need they  wanted somebody who was native to the  problem and to the country to do the  illustrations for this board game  oh I'm also interested in sort of this  shift from some of the more 2d static  artistic formats that you said you're  kind of you're rooted in and how the  interactivity and the collaboration of  this project effects your approach to  covering the topic well it's been really  interesting be never worked on a game  game but a game board before and I  sometimes I hardly even play them even  so it was very interesting to figure out  how to tell a story I could not tell  right now if it's just my okay it looks  like Rosa is frozen it's not just me so  well I think we wait for Rosa to join us  I think I can use this time to sort of  set up the next learn a question that I  have basically I had the wonderful  opportunity to play test the program  it's a board game that the game lab is  working on and then Rosa is contributing  to and the design of it to me is just  really fascinating it takes this complex  story of the law that Nosa was just  describing and puts you in the position  maybe generally the Berto Rican  government trying to make balances  between spending money on infrastructure  and education and paying off its debt to  the US and the debt it's sort of in the  form of these physical jewels that  you're trying to push off of the map but  in order to do that you also need to  spend some of those jewels education  infrastructure Rosa's Beck it's crazy  here yeah I fixed it so hopefully hope  won't happen again all right I was  talking a little bit about my experience  play testing the game or just the way  that it kind of works I think we can  circle back to some of the experience  with the medium as a part of this  question but I was essentially sort of  talking about how one thing that stuck  out to me about this game was how really  closely tied the topic and the the  format are compared to other games that  I've had the chance to test out with the  game lab in their anti-colonial board  game project sort of playing other board  games that approach this topic one thing  that stuck out to me about a lot of  those games is that the colonialist  themes usually have nothing to do with  actual gameplay or the interactions and  rather than make some sort of  educational or aesthetic or emotional  statement about colonialism those games  usually just use the theme as an excuse  to have fun while collecting treasure  and so it was really refreshing for me  to see this board game dive into the  tricky questions of how to educate and  offer a meaningful aesthetic experience  and highlight the emotional experience  of balancing infrastructure and  education and debt at that government  level and so my next question is related  did that and tributing your artwork to  this game I really want to know what  it's like to try to do all those things  at once and have the artwork also help  people learn how to play the game and so  I know you got cut off a little bit and  sort of explaining the how the format  affect to you but I think this can sort  of all be rolled up into one big  question yeah I mean McHale didn't  explain to me how the game was before I  got MIT for the for the quick visit and  I think he was right because you have to  actually experience it because it's uh I  was also kind of expecting it to be like  well I'm gonna educate you about this  and you're gonna roll the dice and  you're gonna get to from point A to  point B and that's it  but I think what they created is very  creative because it puts the person in  the position of having to actually  analyze on how to spend the resources of  a whole country but in a very simple way  so one of my challenges is how to  contextualize that so it's even more  obvious so that when the game asks you  to invest in an infrastructure then  we'll what specific example of Puerto  Rico's history can I use there so that  the person can engage with history as  well as engaged with the game and  because I do comics I'm very used to  I'll walk you through the story but now  the people actually have to like play  the game and it's very cooperative to  like you like you notice so what I think  my challenge is is how to present Porto  Rico in a non stereotypical way how to  contextualize the history so that the  person understands how to play it but at  the same time learn something about how  well if we put the resources from  education to infrastructure and you lose  this and this is going to happen it  could end up in a protest it could end  up with a teacher strike but also one of  the things that I'm very excited to work  on with this project is that make I also  wanted a comic book as the instructions  mmm but then the the comic book is not  only just going to be the instructions  on how to play it'll be a conversation  between two players about the context of  the history of the game so I'm very  excited to to be able to put something  that I'm very comfortable with into the  game at the same time while being  challenged with all of the illustrations  and and kind of like the decisions of  what I want to illustrate there so that  the person takes something away from the  game more than the enjoyment of playing  oh and I think that leads me into my  final question of there's a lot to  unpack in this game I think it'll mean a  lot depending on who plays it in what  context and when even and so my last  question to use  is who do you want to play this game I  want everybody to play the games but I  know that everybody here is super  excited the the moment I said that I was  working on four game about put paprika  people were like but what is it about  what I need to know I do have a  prototype and they were so excited so I  would I would really I would really love  to have once everything is done to a  point I would really love to have a  community after the pandemic after  everything a community plays so that  people here can add to their add their  own stories to the game and and maybe  start like I think this game can can  probably have a type of closure or  healing on some of the of these topics  like oh yeah like they could go oh I  remember when we protested about this or  I remember how I felt when the  government announced this and that but  that they are also working together and  realize that oh yeah if we do invest in  education we might have a way out so it  sounds like a bit of a grim game saying  like oh this is just to push the dead  off the island and the dead is so huge  that it's unpayable and you're gonna  learn that but I think because it's  cooperative it has it has a way to maybe  help form a conversation so that people  can get a little bit of closure and a  little bit of recontextualization on  their feelings and of their history I'm  very excited to play here yeah I'm  excited to play the the final game as  well great thanks so much this was a  really fantastic conversation it was  really inspiring to learn more about  your own approach to this work how the  dialogue around the project and you know  exposure to some of the issues that  you're talking about getting that into  MIT and making that part of the the  conversation around here something that  I really appreciated seeing so thank you  so much and thanks for the thank  everybody for letting me work on this  project  thank you Sam and Rosa and thanks  everyone for joining us for this  conversation please visit art  mit.edu slash from anywhere for a wide  range of arts activities online  exhibitions and performances research  and more also keep an eye on our website  for more information about this board  game and the developments in the future  thanks again everyone and have a  wonderful weekend goodbye
 

